---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { absoluteUrl, seo } from '../../data/seo';

const meta = seo.contribute;
const siteUrl = Astro.site?.toString() || 'https://tensor-standards.pages.dev/';
const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'TechArticle',
  headline: meta.title,
  description: meta.description,
  url: absoluteUrl(siteUrl, meta.canonicalPath),
};
---

<BaseLayout title={meta.title} description={meta.description} canonicalPath={meta.canonicalPath} ogType={meta.ogType} jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <div class="container prose-card">
    <p class="section-kicker">Help make investigations portable</p>
    <h2>Bring a case, an implementation, or a careful review</h2>
    <p>The next stage is proving that shared investigative meaning survives exchange. Contributions are most useful when they expose an ambiguity, demonstrate a real investigation need, or test a boundary between Core and another standard.</p>
    <div class="cta-group"><a class="btn btn-primary" href="https://github.com/tensor-standards-consortium/TENSOR-Framework/issues/new">Open a framework issue</a><a class="btn btn-secondary" href="https://github.com/tensor-standards-consortium/TENSOR-Framework/pulls">Review proposals and code</a></div>

    <h2>Useful first contributions</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Practitioners:</strong> describe a synthetic investigation where an assessment changed, evidence was unavailable, or two investigators disagreed.</li>
      <li><strong>Implementers:</strong> build a reader or exporter independently and report the meaning preserved or lost.</li>
      <li><strong>Researchers:</strong> evaluate whether Core supports review, handoff, and reproducibility in a defined task.</li>
      <li><strong>Standards specialists:</strong> test the CASE/UCO and CACAO boundaries with the reference cases.</li>
      <li><strong>Domain editors:</strong> review a legacy question's proposition, context, evidence requirements, and source quality before proposing a public profile.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Make a proposal reviewable</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Identify the artifact and version, the problem, and the expected behavior.</li>
      <li>Include a minimal synthetic example with both successful and failing cases where relevant.</li>
      <li>Explain compatibility, privacy, and security implications, plus alternatives considered.</li>
      <li>State which Core requirements or binding rules are affected and what remains untested.</li>
      <li>Disclose relevant affiliations and retain source attribution.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>Do not include credentials, confidential incident data, or evidence you cannot share. A public standard does not require public disclosure of investigations. Use the repository's published security-reporting instructions for a vulnerability rather than a public incident-data dump.</p>

    <h2>What happens next</h2>
    <p>Repository review can improve this implementation candidate. Formal stewardship and independent review follow the <a href="/governance/">governance proposal</a> once adopted. A merged contribution is not a certification, partnership, or governance appointment.</p>
    <p><a href="/founding/05-review-plan.md">Review and adoption plan</a> · <a href="/core/">Candidate artifacts</a> · <a href="/questions/">Legacy questions awaiting semantic review</a></p>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
